+# Prior open-bay reading — verbatim historical text, superseded2026-11-03
+
+The source records below are retained exactly as they read before the current closed-shoulder working choice. No dated conversion is implied.
+
+## Prior current research_note — verbatim
+
+VERIFIED 1902 PICTURE SOURCE: trowbridge_green_tree_1902 is W. E. S. Trowbridge’s "The Green Tree Tavern in 1835", independently verified in Edwin O. Gale’s 1902 book, facing printed p. 35. The artwork’s earlier rights hold is superseded by the actual title/copyright publication evidence. Its 1835 caption is retrospective and does not date every feature to 1 July 1835. Original picture comparisons remain open; no dimensions, placement, geometry or confidence values change with this source verification.
+
+PRIOR RESEARCH RECORD:
+THE BEST-DOCUMENTED BUILDING IN THIS DATASET, and it is documented from the inside out — which is the opposite of the usual situation here and is worth understanding. The Green Tree survived until 1902, long enough for people who had slept in it to be asked what it was like in print: John Gray, who kept it 1838-41, Edwin O. Gale, who was put up in a chamber 'about 12x12, with two windows 6x8', and F. H. Taylor, who fixed which street the front was on. From them come the room module, the 7 1/2 ft ceilings, the board partitions, the interior whitewash, the dividing hall and its stairway, the bar-room at one end, the attic used for lodging, the gable-end attic window and the low one-storey additions at each end. What none of them gives is an overall dimension, so even here the footprint is a derivation from the room module rather than a measurement. IT ALSO SURVIVED LONG ENOUGH TO BE PHOTOGRAPHED, about 1859, at its original corner — the closest thing to direct visual evidence for any Wolf Point building. Note the caveat carefully, because the version in circulation is wrong: the 1859 plate PREDATES the 1880 move and shows the tavern where it was built, so what stands between it and the scene date is twenty-six years of alteration, not forty-seven years and a move. That plate has not been seen for this record and it is the single highest-value open lead in the parcel: it would settle the overall dimensions, the exterior finish and the gallery in one look. CONFLICTS RECORDED: frame throughout versus a log core weatherboarded later, a claim attributable to W. H. Stow personally; the carpenter, Philo Cobb versus Silas B. Cobb; the move dated 1880 versus 1881; and, the largest in metres, whether 'Lake and Canal' or 'Lake and West Water' names the corner, which are a full block apart. See docs/RESEARCH/green_tree_tavern.md.
+
+## Prior enclosed_left_annex note — verbatim
+
+The retrospective pictures disagree about the low structure immediately beside the main gable. Trowbridge/Gale, captioned 1835, shows an open post-and-roof wagon bay; Andreas shows a closed shoulder with a sash and small gable light, followed by a low connector and a farther-left gabled range. Trowbridge crops farther left and does not establish that the gabled range was absent. The canonical reading keeps its open bay and reconstructs the connector and farther range; the separate full proposal retains a closed-shoulder alternative; this minimal package enrolls no alternate version. These are competing pictured forms, not a demonstrated alteration history. The later reproduction's 1838 caption is not a date supplied by the original Andreas plate.
+
+The occurrence and architectural categories are inferred from the pictures. Every metric span, unpictured depth, height, slope, opening station/section, local placement, plan turn and timber/color treatment is reconstructed, so the entire numeric group is marked reconstructed. The farther range has a 4 m front, 3 m hidden depth, roughly 4.3 m ridge and 25 degree turn toward the pictured viewpoint; its 1 m front projection and the overall 0.20 m offset are reconstruction choices, not surveyed coordinates. The actual occupied solids remain separate, preserving the open bay between the main building and the connector.
+
+No source proves that these undated additions stood unchanged on 1 July 1835. Taylor's arrival in1834 does not date his remembered lodging in a left addition. Their use in this scene is a declared reconstruction; no tenant identity, authentic interior or extra lodging capacity is inferred. The unverified 1859 catalogue photograph supplies no geometry. See docs/LIBERTIES.md L-green-tree-enclosed-left-reading-2376 and the source qualifications in docs/RESEARCH/green-tree-annex-T-2376/README.md. The modern Chicagology page is retained elsewhere only for transcribed historical testimony and counterevidence, not as a geometry source for this group.
+
+## Prior compound OPEN entry — verbatim
+
+The low connector and turned farther gabled range are now visibly present in the genuine new-model comparison, replacing the former rear ell. This is a partial massing correction, not a match for the whole illustrated left group: Andreas shows a taller enclosed shoulder and dormered mass beside the main gable, while the canonical Trowbridge-led reading leaves the shoulder open and the procedural yard layer supplies its roof/posts outside this asset-only render. The farther range's pictured opening stations/count and dimensions have not been fully matched. These retrospective alternatives do not establish a dated July1835 alteration sequence. Keep this compound discrepancy open on original T-2376; no new photo follow-up or whole-building signoff.
+
+## Prior lower-side sash assessment — verbatim historical text
+
+The middle south-wall doorway is now the pictured stock sash: the actual new-master comparison at the unchanged Andreas camera shows seven lower sash rather than six sash and a middle door. Independent exported-triangle comparison confines the 56 replaced triangles to that opening; the existing 0.75 m sill, 0.85 m glass width and 2.10 m head are retained model controls, not archival measurements. Gray's later side-door recollection remains explicit counterevidence; the picture-led choice is inferred and proves no exact July-1835 absence of a door. Actualpublishedbothviewportbody/yard/ground/NormalByGrade/entry/creditedsourceandnativeWEBreviewpassed2026-10-29 onthe source-boundc093candidate; the substantialenclosedannexremainsOPEN.
